ation system  they will  you can see around here  the  drip lines that are laid on the ground  with the small trees  we actually succeeded last year in  putting in  a  system that has the potential to  irrigate the entire farm  once we have all the blocks  hooked up to it and and the drip tape  laid down in the orchard we're also  gradually putting in a trellis system  that will help  support the small trees we're going to a  more dense  format for the orchard  we have  diversified water sources for our  irrigation  for the same reason that we have so much  rainfall variability  so we do draw from a small creek we have  several ponds  and  and a couple of wells and  in addition to the irrigation  using  this is now more effects the the  greenhouse the vegetable operation but  we're using greenhouses  to  balance for example with the tomatoes  rather than having  our entire tomato crop out in the field  we're putting a portion of it in a  greenhouse  to balance that  and then for the first time  this coming year we will  grow some small fruit  in  a high tunnel so high tunnel looks a lot  like a greenhouse  it's not heated this is the difference  it's not heated  and it's really  a  three season  or even two season but really a three  season uh cover to regulate  uh number one temperature variation and  number two  uh  water or rainfall  so that we can  make sure that the crop for example with  small fruit and cherries is a good  example  regulate  the  water the amount of water that the crop  gets and prevent  um in so doing prevent  the  fungus problems that one would  ordinarily have with those with those  cherries  so from a from a farming point of view i  think the most valuable information we  could get from cornell  is uh on varieties that will be adapted  for the next  20 years  of you know climatic variations  that would be best adapted as we're  planting new  blocks out every every season  an issue that is perhaps most critical  in the whole  issue of adapting to climate change and  that is  management and it's not just  what the choices of what you plant and  how you farm it's also  improving management strategies  and and  keeping a closer control on everything  that you do on the farm record keeping  being very precise apartments  activities  i think that  certainly our  our focus is how we can manage  with what we have  we we're producing a crop a variety of  crops for a fabulous market in new york  city there are lots of people who need  to eat  and  and we're lucky with our location  so  what we're interested in doing is  is adapting to whatever comes our way  and making sure we can manage through  that  we don't feel like as one  farm operation we can necessarily  influence the  the climate change itself we'd rather  manage  the effects that we're feeling  yeah  so on the question of  of whether  climate change is uh  caused by  human influence human activity or not  we  feel like it's  that's not a concern that we are going  to  address here on the farm we are very  much more interested in addressing the  effects of the climate change and  what we can do to  to deal with it  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu
 

